 "notes": [
  "The tutorial text expects H2 about 0.28 in the products. DWSIM gives 0.42 for the Conversion reactor: the 0.32 produced (0.4 x 0.8) is added to the 0.10 already in the feed. The CO value (0.08) agrees with the text.",
  "DWSIM energy flow includes the formation enthalpy: feed -170458 kJ/kmol = sum(z Hf) + sensible 9830.83 = -180288.1 + 9830.83 with Hf(CO) -110525, Hf(H2O g) -241818, Hf(CO2) -393509 kJ/kmol. The 'Molar Enthalpy' rows are sensible only. Reactor duty = energy flow out - energy flow in.",
  "Duty (energy flow of the reactor): conversion -123.811 kW, equilibrium -123.535 kW (from the block), Gibbs -123.96 kW (product minus feed energy flow).",
  "Product streams show phases V+L with a liquid flow of about 1e-14 mol/s: numerical residue, the products are vapor. The streams 'Product-*-Liq' (second outlet) are empty, and Product-Gibbs-Liq holds meaningless values; they are not used.",
  "Equilibrium product vs its own Keq: ln(Ky) from the product mole fractions is 3.2955, sum(nu ln phi) from the stored fugacity coefficients is +0.0111, so ln K = 3.3066 against 4577.8/600 - 4.33 = 3.2997 (difference 0.0069, origin not known: solver tolerance or a different fugacity evaluation).",
  "The Gibbs product implies ln K = 3.3374 (mole fractions plus the same phi), 0.0377 above the Keq expression: DWSIM Gibbs uses its own thermochemistry, not the user expression.",
